# ── Caddyfile — HTTPS in front of Jarvis's dashboard/API ─────────────────────
# Why this exists: the new Loads feature's camera capture uses getUserMedia(),
# which browsers refuse to run outside a "secure context" (https://, or
# http://localhost). Jarvis is currently reached at a bare IP over plain
# http:// (port 8080, no TLS anywhere in the repo or deploy notes) — camera
# buttons will fail with a permission error until this is in front of it.
#
# Caddy auto-provisions and auto-renews a real Let's Encrypt certificate with
# zero manual cert management, as long as a domain name points at this VM.
# It replaces nothing in Jarvis itself — Jarvis keeps running on :8080
# exactly as it does today; Caddy just terminates TLS on :443 and forwards
# to it.
#
# ── REQUIRED before this works: you need a domain (or subdomain) with a DNS
# A record pointing at 35.233.131.198. Don't have one? Two options:
#   1. Cheapest: buy/use any domain you already control, add an A record
#      (e.g. jarvis.yourdomain.com -> 35.233.131.198), replace the line below.
#   2. No domain at all: skip Caddy, use a Cloudflare Tunnel instead — gives
#      you HTTPS on a *.trycloudflare.com URL with no DNS purchase. Ask if
#      you want that path instead; it's a different (also small) setup.
#
# Replace the placeholder domain below, then see the install steps in
# HTTPS_SETUP.md (also added to this repo) for the exact commands to run ON
# THE VM. I don't have SSH access to 35.233.131.198, so I can't run these for
# you — this file and the doc are everything needed once you SSH in.

jarvis.YOURDOMAIN.com {
	reverse_proxy localhost:8080
}
